    id: 'r-coet-n3', skill: 'coet', sub: 'Supporting a claim', d: 'H',
    passage: 'In northern Mozambique, honey hunters of the Yao people seek out greater honeyguides, birds that lead people to wild bees’ nests and afterward feed on the wax the hunters leave behind once they have harvested the honey. Because the hunters attract the birds with a distinctive trilling call, a “brrr-hm” passed down through generations, evolutionary biologist Claire Spottiswoode hypothesized that honeyguides recognize this particular sound as a signal that a human partner is ready to follow them to a nest.',
