    id: 'r-coet-n10', skill: 'coet', sub: 'Supporting a claim', d: 'V',
    passage: 'Male songbirds in many temperate forests begin singing in the darkness before dawn, and in several species the earliest singers are especially successful at fathering offspring outside their own pairs. Ornithologist Bart Kempenaers and colleagues observed that males holding territories near street lights at the edge of a forest began their dawn song earlier than males deeper in the woods, and they hypothesized that the artificial light itself, rather than some other feature of territories along the forest edge, caused the earlier singing.',
